[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Every single one of these camera angles I picked on purpose, and I did it completely for free with this one secret tool. You see, AI video models often struggle to give us precise camera control. And if you think generating a still image first would fix this issue once and for all, then good luck wasting'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'credits twice. So, in this video, I\'ll walk you through the exact free tool I use to get any camera angle I want. Then I\'ll reveal the simple workflow I discovered that skips the whole image-to-video process. And by the end of this video, you\'ll be able to get any'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera angle you can think of while spending the least credits possible. So, if you want to follow along, the links to all the tools and prompts are waiting for you down below. Let\'s dive in. But first, let\'s talk about the most frustrating thing about AI video. So,'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you have a shot in your head. You know exactly how you want the camera to sit. So, you type your prompt, you spend some credits, and you wait. But the model gives you something completely different. And now you\'re thinking that by regenerating again and again, you\'ll'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'eventually get the output you want. But this is completely wrong. By doing this, you\'re not directing anymore. You\'re just gambling with your credits. And I\'ll be honest with you, I did the exact same thing when I started out. So, if you don\'t see what I mean or you haven\'t'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tried this before, let me demonstrate it with one of the hardest shots to pull off, over the shoulder. It\'s brutal for AI because the camera has to sit in one very specific spot, right behind the subject\'s shoulder, looking past the'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'subject\'s face. So, we\'ll generate this sample with C Dance inside Higgs Field. For our prompt, we\'ll simply ask for an over-the-shoulder shot of our subject on a neon-lit street. We hit generate, and here\'s what we got.'

[01:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Instead of looking over the shoulder, we\'re stuck staring at the subject\'s'

[01:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'back the entire time. Now, you might be yelling at your screen for me to just generate an image first and animate that, and I hear you. But that\'s the old image-to-video trick, and it\'s exactly what we\'re going to skip for this one. Because on top of burning extra credits'

[01:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on that image, it becomes the hard first frame of your video, making your shot stiff. Yes, it works on most angles, and it\'s honestly one of the best prompting tricks I recommend in my videos. But to get a difficult camera angle like this one, what we really need is a base the'

[02:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera can actually move from. And that\'s the whole reason I built this free tool. It\'s called Shot Stage. You block the exact camera angle you want in a simple 3D scene, and it locks that angle straight into your AI video. And these frames right here are the secret.'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In a moment, we\'re going to tag this frame inside our prompt, and it\'ll act as the still image for our scene. Except we don\'t spend a single credit making it. But first, let me walk you through the tool. It\'s a simple 3D room with our subject standing in the middle. Think of'

[02:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it as a stand-in for whoever your real character is. We\'re not generating anything yet. We\'re just placing the camera exactly where we want it. And flying around here works just like a video game. We move the camera with the WASD keys. A slides us left, D slides us'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right. W pushes us in toward the subject. S pulls us back out. We drag the mouse to look around and scroll to dolly in for the fine adjustments. Now, here\'s the one that\'s easy to miss. Q lowers the camera and E raises it. But'

[02:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'watch the eye line. We\'re not tilting up or down. We stay perfectly level. We just get shorter or taller. We can sink the camera all the way to the floor or lift it up near the ceiling, and the horizon stays flat the whole time. That tiny bit of height control is what gives'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a shot its mood. Everything else lives over on the right. Up top are our shot presets: medium, wide, close-up, low angle, high angle, over shoulder, and two shot. One click and the camera snaps to that setup for us. Just under that,'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the lens slider sets how wide or tight the lens feels, just like a real camera. Then, down here, we control our subject. We can slide the subject left or right, push it deeper into the room, and rotate it to face any direction we want. And'

[03:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here\'s the part that makes the whole thing work. Every time we touch anything, the readouts up in the corner and along the bottom update live. So, at a glance, we always know exactly what the camera is seeing and which way our subject is facing. That\'s the entire'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tool. Honestly, [music] it takes about 30 seconds to learn, but that\'s just the walk-through. Let me show you what it can actually do. So, let\'s build that over-the-shoulder shot from scratch. The right way this time. We\'ll click the over shoulder preset and the camera'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tucks in behind the subject\'s shoulder instantly. Now, we\'ll press W to move in closer and drag the lens slider so the framing feels more cinematic. And just like that, we\'ve placed the camera in the exact spot we want. Now, we\'ll head over to the prompt box and select C'

[04:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Dance 2.0 as our model. We need to set our model right here. So, Shot Stage writes the prompt in the exact style that model works best with. Then, we\'ll fill in some details about our character. In the subject box, we\'ll describe our character. And in the'

[04:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'environment box, we describe the setting we want them in. For this example, we\'ll keep camera move to push in and style to moody cinematic lighting. Now, we hit generate and the tool gives us a window that says, "Your shot is ready." In here, it already wrote the entire prompt'

[04:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for us and copied it to our clipboard. If you read it, it tells the model to use the blocking frame only as the camera reference to match this exact angle and to ignore the plain gray placeholder. It even baked in the lens and the camera height for us. I\'m'

[04:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'personally really satisfied with this. So, we\'ll download the frame and hit create in Higgsfield. And Shot Stage takes us straight into Higgsfield. Once we\'re in, we\'ll toggle over to video and select C Dance 2.0. We\'ll upload our frame, paste in the prompt Shot Stage'

[04:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gave us, and hit generate. And this is the result we got. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; A clean over the shoulder tucked right'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'behind the subject with her face finally in view, exactly where we placed the camera on Shot Stage earlier. This is already miles away from the back of the head mess we got at the start. But honestly, this is just one shot. The real deal is when we lock our subject'

[05:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and our whole environment in place, so every angle we make stays perfectly consistent. And it all comes down to these two checkboxes: I have a character sheet and I have an environment sheet. In a real project, you should always have these. I\'ve already generated both,'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so all I\'m going to do is tick these two boxes. And once we check them, Shot Stage adapts the prompt for us. It stops trying to describe our character with words and instead tells the model to use our sheets. In this way, our character stays the exact same person and our'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'setting stays the exact same place across every angle we shoot. So, let\'s rebuild that same over-the-shoulder shot, but this time the proper way. We block it in Shot Stage, set our camera move to push in, and our look/style to moody cinematic lighting. Then, we hit'

[06:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'download frame and click create in Higgs Field. Now, we\'re in Higgs Field and here\'s where the sheets come in. We\'ll upload three things: our environment sheet, our character sheet, and our blocking frame. Then, we paste our prompt and tag each one with the @ symbol, [music] so the model knows'

[06:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exactly which is which. We tag our frame for the angle. Then, we tag our character sheet for the subject, and we tag our environment sheet for the location. And honestly, it\'s that easy. After that, we hit generate and here\'s what we got.'

[06:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'A clean over-the-shoulder, the same subject, the same street, locked exactly'

[06:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to our frame. Now, let\'s prove this really works by running every angle, one after another and side-by-side with the frame. First, a low angle. In Shot Stage, we click low angle and the camera drops below the eyeline, looking up. We keep both sheet boxes ticked, set our'

[06:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera move to push in, and our look/style to moody cinematic lighting. Then, we\'ll hit generate. From here, we download our frame and click create in Higgs Field. We\'ll paste our prompt, upload our references, and tag them just'

[07:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like we did earlier. And here\'s the result. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; On the left is the blocking frame from'

[07:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Shot Stage, and on the right is the video we got. She towers over us exactly the way a low angle should feel. Now, a close-up. We click close-up and the camera moves in tight. Both boxes still ticked, we set our camera move to orbit and keep the look/style on moody'

[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cinematic lighting. Then, we generate, download the frame, and hit create in Higgs Field again. At this point, you\'re already a master at this. So, we\'ll paste our prompt, upload the references, and tag them one by one. And here\'s what we got.'

[07:48] [scene:talk]
